Host-side tests for the REL file support
========================================

This directory contains a small test harness that compiles firmware
sources for the host by shadowing the AVR-specific headers with the
shims in shims/. stubs.c supplies the remaining firmware environment
(error channel, command buffer and image loading), and the disk images
are ordinary host files.

The harness covers both REL back-ends:

  - D64 (src/d64ops.c), which harness.c includes directly to reach its
    internal functions.
  - FAT (src/fatops.c and the rest of the file layer), driven through
    the full file_open() path. fatshim.c provides the FatFs API on top
    of libc so the unmodified fatops.c runs on the host.

harness.c emulates the bus protocol of iec.c (refill on EOI and on
buffer wrap for REL files). The FAT tests fill the command buffer and
call file_open(), which runs the same filename and record length parsing
the command channel uses.

Running the tests
-----------------

    make check      generates the test images (blank.d64 and multi.d64
                    via d64lib.py, blank.d71, blank.d81 and c1541.d64 via
                    the c1541 tool from VICE, looked up on PATH; set
                    C1541=... to use a different binary), then
                    runs the tests twice (plain and with ASan/UBSan) and
                    verifies the resulting images with validate.py
    make validate   alias for check
    make clean      removes build products and test images

Working images and the FAT root live under the directory given to the
harness (testdata/ by default, also the default of validate.py) and are
recreated as needed.

Coverage
--------

The D64 tests cover creating REL files, reading and writing records
(including records that span sector boundaries and record length 254),
seeking with the P command, growth past the end of the file, appending,
reopening (including the record length mismatch error), scratch on a
multi-sector directory, linked buffers, read-only images, files without
data sectors (opening, record length mismatch, growth and scratch) and
reading a file created by c1541 itself.

The D81 test grows a file across a super side sector group boundary, the
D71 tests cover the six side sector limit and the last record at it.

The FAT tests drive the same read, write, seek and growth paths through
file_open() with the FatFs shim, plus the storage layouts: extension
based R00 files with the P00-style header (record length at offset 25)
and the raw layout with a single record length byte. Where FAT behaves
differently from the D64 path, the so marked QUIRK tests assert the
current behaviour.

validate.py checks the on-disk side sector and data sector chains, BAM
allocation and directory entry contents, and cross-checks selected
files with c1541.

Note that these tests exercise the REL implementation logic; they do not
replace a firmware build. Compile for real hardware with "make CONFIG=..."
before flashing.
